meta { title = "Modules and exports", description = "Organize source into explicit modules and imports.", order = 10, group = "Language reference" }

Each .k file is a module. Declarations are private unless exported. Imports name exactly which declarations enter the current scope, with optional aliases. Relative imports include their .k extension.

h2 { "Export a function" }

In math.k:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """export fn double(value: i64) -> i64:
  return value * 2
""" }

In main.k in the same directory:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

from "./math.k" import double as twice

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  output: i64 = twice(21)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra-check main.k
kalandra main.k -o program
./program
""" }

The executable displays 42. The import path is relative to main.k, not to the terminal's working directory. Keep both files in the same directory for this example.

h2 { "Call a Kalandra function from C or C++" }

Use export "C" to give a function a public C symbol. The same function can be imported by another Kalandra module. Give it a library-specific name so other libraries can link alongside it.

In geometry.k:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """export "C" fn geometry_twice(value: i32) -> i32:
  return value * 2

export "C" direct fn geometry_scale(value: f64) -> f64:
  return value * 2.0
""" }

Build a static library and its matching header:

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra geometry.k --emit static --header geometry.h -o libgeometry.a
""" }

In caller.c:

codeblock { language = c, """#include "geometry.h"
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
  int32_t result = 17;
  if (geometry_twice(INT32_MAX, &result) != KALANDRA_C_FAULT || result != 17)
    return 1;
  if (geometry_twice(21, &result) != KALANDRA_C_OK)
    return 1;
  printf("%.0f", geometry_scale(result / 2.0));
  return 0;
}
""" }

codeblock { language = bash, """cc caller.c libgeometry.a -o caller
./caller
""" }

The caller prints 42. The generated header also works in C++. Use object or shared in place of static to produce a relocatable object or a shared library for the target platform.

By default, an exported C function returns an int32_t status. For a value result, pass a writable pointer as the final argument. On success the function writes the value and returns KALANDRA_C_OK. On an execution fault, such as signed overflow, it returns KALANDRA_C_FAULT and leaves the output untouched. A null output pointer returns KALANDRA_C_INVALID_ARGUMENT. For a unit result the caller receives just the status. Parameters can use fixed-width integers, f32, f64 or bool.

Use export "C" direct fn when a C API requires a function that returns its value directly. The compiler must prove that the body can run without a Kalandra execution context or a fault. A direct export returning unit has the C return type void.

h2 { "Pass a callback to C" }

A C function can borrow a named Kalandra callback for the duration of the call. Its header declares a non-null function pointer with Clang’s noescape parameter attribute. For example, a native event library can accept a callback that maps an event number to an i32.

codeblock { language = c, """#include <stdint.h>
typedef int32_t (*Event)(int32_t);
int32_t dispatch(Event _Nonnull handler __attribute__((noescape)), int32_t event);
""" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "events" import dispatch
from "std/process" import ExitStatus

export "C" direct fn eventNumber(value: i32) -> i32:
  return value

fn main() -> ExitStatus:
  if dispatch(eventNumber, 17) == 17:
    return ExitStatus {code: 0}
  return ExitStatus {code: 1}
""" }

Here C calls the function with an i32 and receives its result directly. The compiler checks the signature and proves that the function can complete without a fault. The native implementation must finish using the function pointer before dispatch returns. This form accepts scalar callbacks without captured state.

h2 { "Export types deliberately" }

Record, enum and error declarations can be exported and imported. Aliasing preserves their nominal identity rather than creating a new type. A helper continues resolving its own private names in its declaring module; importing it does not expose all of that module.

h2 { "Re-export a public surface" }

An explicit re-export lets a library entry expose selected functions or types from internal modules. Re-exports preserve the original declaration identity and helper scope. Use a small public entry instead of requiring callers to know the internal directory layout.

h2 { "Use a package name" }

A bare import such as from "geometry" import total refers to an explicit package mapping or installed dependency. The compiler does not guess a module from your working directory or fetch a missing library while building.

